<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wu Sangui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1612">1612</a>–<a href="/years/1678">1678</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wu Sangui was a Ming general who commanded the defences at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanhai-pass">Shanhai Pass</a> in 1644. After rebels captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, he allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> forces through the pass and joined them, enabling the Qing conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He was later granted a large southern fiefdom, and his revolt as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-three-feudatories">Revolt of the Three Feudatories</a> was suppressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wu-sangui.jpg" alt="Wu Sangui" width="283" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author in Qing Dynasty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wu_Sangui.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In April 1644 Li Zicheng's rebels captured Beijing and the Chongzhen Emperor took his own life; the Ming general <b>Wu Sangui</b> then allied with the Manchus, who defeated Li at Shanhai Pass and occupied Beijing, establishing the Qing dynasty there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The decisive breakthrough came in 1644 when the Ming general <b>Wu Sangui</b>, facing the rebel army of Li Zicheng, which had just seized Beijing, allowed Manchu forces through the Shanhai Pass.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1658 and 1662 Ming loyalist forces, following the fugitive Yongli Emperor into Burma, raided the country, and in 1662 a Qing army under <b>Wu Sangui</b> reached Ava, withdrawing only after the Burmese court handed over the emperor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Towards the end of the Ming the wall helped hold back the Manchus at the heavily fortified Shanhai Pass, until in 1644, after Beijing had fallen to Li Zicheng’s Shun dynasty, the general <b>Wu Sangui</b> opened its gates to them.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>At the eastern end of the Great Wall the Qing prince-regent Dorgon, allied with the former Ming general <b>Wu Sangui</b>, defeated Li Zicheng, founder of the Shun dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-27">27 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ming general <b>Wu Sangui</b> at the Shanhai Pass allied with the Manchus, and their combined forces defeated Li in May 1644.</blockquote><a href="/state/shun-dynasty">Shun dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Suizhong County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengyang">Hengyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.32,120.34&amp;year=1654" aria-label="Suizhong County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7507 722 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wu Sangui"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7957 992h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7957 992h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.32,120.34&amp;year=1654">What was here in 1654? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
